WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.672
This query first trims the email and converts it to lowercase.

00:00:03.672 --> 00:00:08.252
SPLIT_PART then returns the text before or after the at sign.

00:00:08.252 --> 00:00:11.507
POSITION reports where the delimiter occurs.

00:00:11.507 --> 00:00:15.742
Run the query and compare the parsed user and domain values.

00:00:15.742 --> 00:00:20.532
Also notice what happens for missing or malformed email values.

00:00:20.532 --> 00:00:25.402
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:25.402 --> 00:00:31.567
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:34.017
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:39.112
In a real setting such as a CRM standardizing names, emails, and phone numbers,

00:00:39.112 --> 00:00:46.392
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:46.392 --> 00:00:48.757
before discussing syntax.

00:00:48.757 --> 00:00:55.163
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
